        },
        "question": {
          ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/ca9837-templates-5ek4tgt5/17592186152187/ellipse-692f393993e0f84f26522a54.png)\n\nThe ellipse shown is centered at the origin. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $J$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The intercepts are $(\\pm6,0)$ and $(0,\\pm2)$, so the horizontal radius is $6$ and the vertical radius is $2$. Each radius is measured from the center, rather than across the whole ellipse.\n\nAn ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=6$ and $b=2$, so\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $J(6,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (6,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,2)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-6,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice.",
